const class12QuestionExpansion: Record<number, {q:string;options:string[];answer:number;why:string}> = {"101":{"q":"Which structure guides the pollen tube towards the embryo sac?","options":["Synergid with its filiform apparatus","Antipodal cell","Anther wall","Seed coat"],"answer":0,"why":"The synergids lie beside the egg and their filiform apparatus helps guide and receive the pollen tube."},"102":{"q":"A plant with genotype Tt forms which types of gametes, in equal expected proportions?","options":["T and t","TT and tt","Tt only","T, t and Tt"],"answer":0,"why":"The two alleles segregate during meiosis, so each gamete receives either T or t."},"103":{"q":"Which enzyme joins adjacent Okazaki fragments during DNA replication?","options":["DNA ligase","Helicase","Primase","RNA polymerase"],"answer":0,"why":"DNA ligase seals breaks in the sugar-phosphate backbone, joining the fragments."},"104":{"q":"Which evolutionary process introduces alleles into a population from another population?","options":["Gene flow","Genetic drift","Natural selection only","Assortative mating"],"answer":0,"why":"Gene flow occurs when individuals or their gametes move between populations and transfer alleles."},"105":{"q":"Which immune cells can differentiate into antibody-secreting plasma cells?","options":["B lymphocytes","Red blood cells","Platelets","Neutrophils only"],"answer":0,"why":"Activated B lymphocytes can differentiate into plasma cells that secrete antibodies."},"106":{"q":"Which microbial process produces methane during anaerobic digestion?","options":["Methanogenesis","Nitrification","Alcoholic oxidation in air","Photosynthesis"],"answer":0,"why":"Methanogenic archaea produce methane from suitable substrates under anaerobic conditions."},"107":{"q":"What is the role of a selectable marker in a cloning vector?","options":["Identify cells that have taken up the vector","Translate the inserted gene","Cut DNA at every sequence","Make the host cell photosynthetic"],"answer":0,"why":"Selectable markers, such as antibiotic-resistance genes, help distinguish cells carrying the vector."},"108":{"q":"In Bt crops, Cry proteins are activated mainly in the insect gut because of its","options":["Alkaline conditions and proteases","High oxygen concentration","Acidic pH only","Absence of digestive enzymes"],"answer":0,"why":"The alkaline insect midgut helps solubilise the protoxin, and gut proteases process it into an active toxin."},"109":{"q":"Which parameter describes the maximum population size an environment can sustain?","options":["Carrying capacity","Natality","Immigration","Biotic potential only"],"answer":0,"why":"Carrying capacity, represented by K, reflects environmental resource limits and conditions."},"110":{"q":"Why is the pyramid of energy always upright?","options":["Energy is lost at each trophic transfer","Producers always have less energy than consumers","Decomposers create energy","Energy is recycled completely"],"answer":0,"why":"Only a fraction of energy passes to the next trophic level; much is used in metabolism or lost as heat."},"111":{"q":"Which is an example of ex-situ conservation?","options":["A seed bank","A national park protecting a forest","A wildlife sanctuary","A biosphere reserve"],"answer":0,"why":"Ex-situ conservation protects biodiversity outside its natural habitat, for example in seed banks and botanical gardens."},"112":{"q":"Which substance begins protein digestion in the human stomach?","options":["Pepsin","Salivary amylase","Bile salts","Maltase"],"answer":0,"why":"Pepsin is a stomach protease that begins protein digestion in the acidic gastric environment."},"113":{"q":"Most exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide in human lungs occurs across the","options":["Alveolar-capillary membrane","Tracheal cartilage","Pleural membrane","Diaphragm tendon"],"answer":0,"why":"Thin alveolar walls lie close to capillaries, providing a large, short diffusion pathway for gas exchange."},"114":{"q":"Which chamber pumps oxygenated blood into the aorta?","options":["Left ventricle","Right ventricle","Right atrium","Left atrium"],"answer":0,"why":"The left ventricle generates pressure to send oxygenated blood through the aorta to the body."},"115":{"q":"Which part of the nephron is the main site of ultrafiltration?","options":["Renal corpuscle","Collecting duct","Loop of Henle only","Distal convoluted tubule"],"answer":0,"why":"Blood is filtered from glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule in the renal corpuscle."},"116":{"q":"Which connective tissue attaches a skeletal muscle to a bone?","options":["Tendon","Ligament","Cartilage","Synovial fluid"],"answer":0,"why":"Tendons connect muscles to bones; ligaments connect bone to bone."},"117":{"q":"At a chemical synapse, neurotransmitter is released from the","options":["Presynaptic axon terminal","Myelin sheath","Nucleus of the postsynaptic cell","Node of Ranvier only"],"answer":0,"why":"An arriving action potential triggers vesicle fusion at the presynaptic terminal and neurotransmitter release."},"118":{"q":"Which hormone lowers blood glucose by promoting uptake and storage?","options":["Insulin","Glucagon","Adrenaline","Thyroxine"],"answer":0,"why":"Insulin promotes glucose uptake in responsive tissues and supports glycogen and other nutrient storage."},"119":{"q":"Which cells can retain immunological memory after an adaptive immune response?","options":["Memory B and T lymphocytes","Mature red blood cells","Platelets","All epithelial cells"],"answer":0,"why":"Memory B and T cells can persist and respond more rapidly when the same antigen is encountered again."},"120":{"q":"In humans, fertilisation most commonly occurs in the","options":["Ampullary region of the oviduct","Cervix","Vagina","Uterine fundus"],"answer":0,"why":"Fertilisation usually occurs at the ampullary-isthmic region of the fallopian tube."},"121":{"q":"Which statement best describes informed consent in reproductive healthcare?","options":["A voluntary decision made with adequate information","A decision made under pressure","Permission assumed from silence","A choice based only on rumours"],"answer":0,"why":"Informed consent requires understandable information, voluntariness and respect for the person's decision."},"122":{"q":"Crossing over occurs between","options":["Non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes","Sister chromatids of unrelated chromosomes","Two bacterial plasmids only","Two non-homologous chromosomes in mitosis"],"answer":0,"why":"During prophase I, non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes exchange corresponding segments."},"123":{"q":"Which term describes the formation of new species?","options":["Speciation","Translation","Osmosis","Glycolysis"],"answer":0,"why":"Speciation is the evolutionary process through which populations become reproductively distinct lineages."},"124":{"q":"What should guide the choice of an applied biological solution?","options":["Evidence, safety and local context","Popularity alone","Ignoring possible side effects","A promise of guaranteed success"],"answer":0,"why":"Applied biology should be evaluated using evidence, safety, effectiveness and the needs of the people and environment involved."}};
const class12PlainLanguage: Record<number,string> = {"101":"A flower makes pollen in its anthers and keeps its egg-containing embryo sac inside an ovule. After pollen reaches the stigma, a pollen tube grows towards the ovule. Two male cells are delivered: one joins the egg to begin the embryo, and the other helps make the food-storing endosperm. This special two-fusion event is called double fertilisation.","102":"Genes come in different versions called alleles. You receive one allele from each parent. When reproductive cells are made, the two alleles separate, so each egg or pollen grain carries just one. A Punnett square helps us predict the possible combinations in offspring.","103":"DNA is the cell's long-term instruction book. Its four bases form a sequence that stores information. A cell can copy DNA before division, read a gene to make RNA, and use RNA instructions to build a protein. The order of DNA bases matters because it helps determine the protein made.","104":"Evolution means that inherited features in a population change over many generations. New variation can arise through mutation and recombination. Natural selection favours traits that improve reproductive success in a particular environment, while chance and movement between populations can also change allele frequencies.","105":"The body protects itself in layers. Skin and mucus help keep germs out. If a pathogen enters, immune cells recognise and respond to it. Some B cells make antibodies, some T cells coordinate or kill infected cells, and memory cells help the body respond faster next time.","106":"Microbes are not all harmful. Some turn milk into curd, help bread rise, make medicines, clean wastewater or produce biogas. Their usefulness depends on the species and the conditions in which they grow.","107":"Biotechnology can move a useful DNA sequence into a host cell. Scientists cut DNA at selected sites, join the desired gene to a vector such as a plasmid, introduce it into host cells, select successful cells and grow them under controlled conditions.","108":"Biotechnology can help make medicines, diagnose disease and improve crops. For example, engineered microbes can produce human insulin and some crops can be made resistant to particular insect pests. These products still need careful testing for safety and effectiveness.","109":"A population is a group of the same species living in one area. Its size changes when individuals are born, die, arrive or leave. Food, water, space, predators and disease can limit growth. A population may grow quickly at first and then slow as resources become scarce.","110":"An ecosystem contains living organisms and the non-living surroundings they interact with. Energy enters mainly through sunlight, moves from producers to consumers and decomposers, and some is lost as heat at every step. Nutrients, unlike energy, can be reused through cycles.","111":"Biodiversity means the variety of life at genetic, species and ecosystem levels. Habitat loss, overuse, invasive species and other pressures can reduce it. Conservation protects species in their natural habitats or safeguards them elsewhere, for example in seed banks and botanical gardens.","112":"Digestion turns food into small molecules that the body can absorb. Food is chewed and mixed with saliva, moved down the food pipe, churned in the stomach and digested further in the small intestine. The liver, gall bladder and pancreas add helpful secretions. Nutrients are absorbed mainly through the intestinal lining.","113":"Breathing brings air into and out of the lungs. When the diaphragm contracts and moves down, the chest cavity expands and air enters. In tiny air sacs called alveoli, oxygen diffuses into the blood and carbon dioxide diffuses from the blood into the air.","114":"The heart is a four-chamber pump. The right side sends oxygen-poor blood to the lungs. The left side receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs and pumps it to the body. Valves help blood move in one direction, and the septum keeps the two sides separate.","115":"The kidneys clean the blood and help control water and salt balance. Each kidney contains many nephrons. Blood is filtered at the glomerulus, useful substances and much water are taken back into the blood, and selected wastes are added to the forming urine.","116":"Bones support the body and protect organs. Muscles create movement by pulling on bones through tendons. Joints allow bones to move relative to one another, while ligaments help hold bones together. At a microscopic level, muscle fibres shorten when actin and myosin filaments slide past one another.","117":"The nervous system sends fast messages. A neuron receives information through its dendrites, carries an electrical impulse along its axon and passes a message to another cell at a synapse. Some responses, such as pulling away from a painful stimulus, can be coordinated quickly through the spinal cord.","118":"Endocrine glands release hormones into the blood. A hormone travels around the body but affects only cells with suitable receptors. Hormones help regulate growth, metabolism, reproduction, stress responses and blood glucose, keeping internal conditions within useful limits.","119":"The immune system identifies and responds to harmful microbes and abnormal cells. Some cells engulf invaders, while lymphocytes recognise specific antigens. B cells can make antibodies, T cells coordinate or carry out cell-mediated responses, and memory cells help protect against future encounters.","120":"The male reproductive system makes and delivers sperm; the female reproductive system produces oocytes and supports development. If a sperm meets an oocyte in the oviduct, fertilisation forms a zygote. The early embryo divides as it travels towards the uterus, where implantation may occur.","121":"Reproductive health includes physical, emotional and social well-being. Reliable information, consent, contraception, prevention and testing for sexually transmitted infections, and access to qualified healthcare all support safe and informed decisions.","122":"Genetics explains how traits are passed on and why variation occurs. Chromosomes carry genes, and different alleles can be inherited from parents. Meiosis reshuffles alleles through independent assortment and crossing over, while fertilisation brings parental genetic material together.","123":"Evolution is the history of how populations and species change over time. Fossils, body structures, geographic patterns and DNA comparisons provide clues. Populations may become different when selection, mutation, chance and reduced gene flow act over many generations.","124":"Applied biology uses biological knowledge to solve practical problems in health, farming, industry and conservation. A good solution is not just clever: it should be tested, safe, effective and suitable for the people and environment where it will be used."};

